## Availability Nines — What They Mean in Practice

```
Availability | Downtime/Year | Downtime/Month | Downtime/Week
-------------|---------------|----------------|---------------
99%          | 3.65 days     | 7.3 hours      | 1.7 hours
99.9%        | 8.7 hours     | 43.8 minutes   | 10.1 minutes
99.95%       | 4.4 hours     | 21.9 minutes   | 5 minutes
99.99%       | 52.6 minutes  | 4.4 minutes    | 1 minute
99.999%      | 5.25 minutes  | 26 seconds     | 6 seconds

Requirements:
  Internal tools: 99% is fine (people go home at night)
  Customer-facing CRUD: 99.9% (SLAs with customers)
  E-commerce checkout: 99.99% (every minute of downtime = lost revenue)
  Banking/payments: 99.999% (regulatory requirements)

Cost increases non-linearly:
  99% → 99.9%: moderate (multi-instance, monitoring)
  99.9% → 99.99%: significant (multi-AZ, chaos testing)
  99.99% → 99.999%: very expensive (multi-region, massive ops team)
```

## Kubernetes Health Checks

```yaml
apiVersion: apps/v1
kind: Deployment
spec:
  replicas: 3  # Always run 3 instances
  template:
    spec:
      containers:
      - name: order-service
        image: order-service:latest

        # Liveness probe: is the container alive? If not, restart it.
        livenessProbe:
          httpGet:
            path: /actuator/health/liveness
            port: 8080
          initialDelaySeconds: 60  # Wait for JVM startup
          periodSeconds: 10        # Check every 10 seconds
          failureThreshold: 3      # Restart after 3 consecutive failures

        # Readiness probe: is it ready to receive traffic?
        # Failed readiness → removed from load balancer (not restarted)
        readinessProbe:
          httpGet:
            path: /actuator/health/readiness
            port: 8080
          initialDelaySeconds: 30
          periodSeconds: 5
          failureThreshold: 2      # Remove from LB after 2 failures

        # Startup probe: allow slow startup without triggering liveness
        startupProbe:
          httpGet:
            path: /actuator/health/liveness
            port: 8080
          initialDelaySeconds: 10
          periodSeconds: 10
          failureThreshold: 30    # 30 × 10s = 5min startup budget
```

```java
// Spring Boot Actuator health endpoints:
@Component
public class DatabaseHealthIndicator implements HealthIndicator {

    private final DataSource dataSource;

    @Override
    public Health health() {
        try (Connection conn = dataSource.getConnection()) {
            conn.prepareStatement("SELECT 1").execute();
            return Health.up()
                .withDetail("database", "PostgreSQL")
                .withDetail("status", "Accepting connections")
                .build();
        } catch (Exception e) {
            return Health.down()
                .withException(e)
                .withDetail("error", "Cannot connect to database")
                .build();
        }
    }
}
// If DB check fails → readiness probe fails → pod removed from LB
// Pod NOT restarted (liveness still OK) — it's a downstream issue
```

## Active-Active vs Active-Passive

```
ACTIVE-PASSIVE (Failover):
  Region A (primary, handling traffic)
  Region B (standby, replicating data, not serving traffic)

  If Region A fails:
    → DNS failover to Region B (can take 1-5 minutes)
    → Region B starts serving traffic
    → During failover: downtime (from seconds to minutes depending on setup)

  Good for: simpler to implement and reason about
  Downtime during failover: yes (RPO/RTO consideration)

ACTIVE-ACTIVE:
  Region A handles US traffic (50%)
  Region B handles EU traffic (50%)

  If Region A fails:
    → DNS routes all traffic to Region B immediately
    → No downtime (already serving traffic)
    → Region B must handle double load (provision for this)

  Harder: data replication must be bidirectional and conflict-free
  Read-only active-active: easy (replicas in each region, writes to single primary)
  Read-write active-active: hard (need CRDTs or careful conflict resolution)

FOR MOST TEAMS: Active-passive across AZs (within same region) is sufficient.
  - All within same AWS region (us-east-1)
  - Services spread across 3 AZs (availability zones)
  - If AZ goes down: other 2 continue serving, K8s restarts pods elsewhere
  - This gives ~99.99% availability with manageable complexity
```

## Graceful Degradation

```java
@Service
public class ProductPageService {

    private final ProductRepository productRepo;
    private final RecommendationService recommendationService;
    private final ReviewService reviewService;

    // Recommendations are non-critical — degrade gracefully if they fail:
    public ProductPageData getProductPage(Long productId) {
        Product product = productRepo.findById(productId).orElseThrow();

        // Fetch recommendations with circuit breaker fallback:
        List<Product> recommendations;
        try {
            recommendations = recommendationService.getRelated(productId);
        } catch (Exception e) {
            log.warn("Recommendations unavailable for product {}, using fallback", productId);
            recommendations = productRepo.findTopSellers(10);  // Generic fallback
        }

        // Fetch reviews with timeout fallback:
        Optional<ReviewSummary> reviews;
        try {
            reviews = reviewService.getSummary(productId);
        } catch (Exception e) {
            log.warn("Reviews unavailable for product {}", productId);
            reviews = Optional.empty();  // Page renders without reviews
        }

        return ProductPageData.builder()
            .product(product)
            .recommendations(recommendations)  // Generic OR personalized
            .reviews(reviews.orElse(null))     // Null → UI shows 'Reviews loading...'
            .build();
    }
}
```

## Production Reality

For an intermediate developer, knowing the Kubernetes liveness/readiness probe distinction is high-value: liveness failure restarts the pod (fixes crashes), readiness failure removes it from the load balancer (handles temporary overload or dependency failure). Getting this wrong causes cascading restart loops in production. Also: graceful shutdown is part of HA — configure `terminationGracePeriodSeconds` and handle `SIGTERM` in your application to allow in-flight requests to complete before the pod stops.
